--TEST--
Core\Csrf::verify refuses every single-character mutation of a token — substituted inside base64's alphabet, substituted outside it, or deleted — at every position of every region, and none of the three sweeps throws — ADR 0060 §§ 1, 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Invariance over a sweep, counted. A token is a nonce, a sealed binding and a
// tag in one string, and a `verify` that compared a prefix, or a decode that
// stopped at the last whole group and ignored what followed, answers correctly
// for every mutation that lands in the region it does read. So the question is
// asked at *every* position rather than at a chosen one, and the count is what
// says the tail was covered. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/csrf.rs` owns why a
// forgery is `false` rather than a throw; the token's width is the sibling
// case `csrf-binds-every-shape-of-session-and-its-length-is-stated`'s.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
string $session = "sid-ada";
string $token = Core\Csrf::issue($session, $key);
int $width = Core\Str::length($token) as int;

// Three mutations at each position: one that keeps the string decodable, one
// that does not, and one that shortens it. The first is the tag check's
// question, the second is the decode's, and ADR 0060 § 1 wants one answer to
// both — a member that distinguished them would tell a forger which half of
// the guess landed.
int $inAlphabet = 0;
int $outsideIt = 0;
int $deleted = 0;
int $threw = 0;
int $i = 0;
while ($i < $width) {
    string $head = Core\Str::slice($token, 0, $i);
    string $tail = Core\Str::slice($token, $i + 1);
    string $swap = "A";
    if (Core\Str::at($token, $i) == "A") {
        $swap = "B";
    }
    try {
        if (Core\Csrf::verify($head . $swap . $tail, $session, $key) == false) {
            $inAlphabet = $inAlphabet + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Csrf::verify($head . "!" . $tail, $session, $key) == false) {
            $outsideIt = $outsideIt + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Csrf::verify($head . $tail, $session, $key) == false) {
            $deleted = $deleted + 1;
        }
    } catch (Throwable $e) {
        $threw = $threw + 1;
    }
    $i = $i + 1;
}

echo "substituted ", $inAlphabet, " of ", $width, " refused", "\n";
echo "unspellable ", $outsideIt, " of ", $width, " refused", "\n";
echo "shortened ", $deleted, " of ", $width, " refused", "\n";
echo "threw ", $threw, "\n";

// And the sweep is not vacuous in either direction. A `verify` answering
// `false` to everything would pass all four lines above, so the unmutated
// token is asked last: it is the same string the sweep was built from, and it
// still verifies.
if (Core\Csrf::verify($token, $session, $key)) {
    echo "and the token it was built from still verifies", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
substituted 79 of 79 refused
unspellable 79 of 79 refused
shortened 79 of 79 refused
threw 0
and the token it was built from still verifies
